    {
      "id": "claim_7ed9040c55a64d92b2d10f27c2f4892b",
      "statement": "Every Typert remote call resolves to the discriminated envelope { ok: true, value: T } | { ok: false, error: { code, message, details } }. The client unwrap() helper collapses it by throwing new Error(r.error?.message ?? 'remote call failed') when r.ok is false, and returning r.value otherwise. Capability views build their api objects by wrapping each ctx.remote.<ns> call in unwrap(), so a host failure surfaces as a Promise rejection the view renders inline.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/client/index.ts#L36-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:be8135f8974d987586c664b44346cf9e9ac650411feededd99d7e38253c34b9a:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/client/index.ts#L99-L132",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:06b92368fe4a8dfc7737177f2c0af3980f6a4cf8426cbd0201fa62b38f4dae96:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_60c80f04dc2640139f391400bf7e2028",
      "statement": "The Typert boundary rejects non-JSON-safe values via assertJsonValue, including a plain object that has an own property with value undefined. zod .optional() parsing leaves such undefined-valued own properties in place instead of deleting the key, so a host method that reconstructs an object from persisted data where an optional member is genuinely absent (pervasive in the team chat path, where chatMessage.agent is optional and absent on user/system messages) would fail boundary validation. compact() strips undefined-valued properties from results before returning; it is applied in loadThread as it decodes each persisted message in src/index.ts#L1123-L1150.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/client/remote.ts#L84-L90",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a51a084be03da068d1ec86b2d5ebc87ab726bad0a32eafeb8fe94f0fbb663bdd:"
